<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Tahirid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">821 CE – 873 CE · capital Nishapur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first hereditary governors of Khurasan under the Abbasids, founded by the general who won al-Ma'mun his throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahir-ibn-husayn">Tahir ibn Husayn</a>, a Persian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasani</a> origin, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin's</a> armies in the civil war of 811 to 813 and secured the throne for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. In 821 al-Ma'mun appointed him governor of Khurasan. Tahir dropped the caliph's name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> shortly before his death in 822, but his family was confirmed in office.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tahirids ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> as loyal but autonomous governors, sending tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> while another branch held the police command of Baghdad itself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-tahir">Abdallah ibn Tahir</a> (828 to 845) was known for justice, protection of peasants and regulation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanat</a> irrigation system. The family were Arabic-speaking patrons of poetry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholarship, while campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> and frontier peoples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tahir">Muhammad ibn Tahir</a> the dynasty weakened, and in 873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>. Tahirids remained prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> into the early 10th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a> later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tahirid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tahirid dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab League · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahirid_Dynasty_821_-_873_(AD).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>821 CE – 873 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nishapur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tahir ibn Husayn, Abdallah ibn Tahir, Muhammad ibn Tahir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tahirids, Tahirid emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=847&amp;focus=tahirid-dynasty" aria-label="Tahirid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6160 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tahirid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6480 1127L6134 1224M6480 1127L6600 1305M6480 1127L6316 1118"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6600 1305h0M6316 1118h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6480 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6480 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nishapur. <a href="/atlas?year=847&amp;focus=tahirid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

